<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bogd Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1869">1869</a>–<a href="/years/1924">1924</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as 8th Jebtsundamba Khutuktu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bogd Khan was the 8th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebtsundamba-khutughtu">Jebtsundamba Khutuktu</a>, the head of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. When Qing rule collapsed in 1911, Mongol nobles proclaimed him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> of an independent Mongolia, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulaanbaatar">Urga</a>. He remained a largely nominal monarch under Chinese occupation and then the 1921 revolution, and after his death the Mongolian People's Republic was declared.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bogd-khan.jpg" alt="Bogd Khan" width="320" height="526" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bogd_Khan_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
